Web Security Write-Up: WAF Bypass & SQLi Behavior Analysis on Enterprise Search Endpoints
1. Executive Summary

During a controlled security assessment of the search infrastructure at Monash University (www.monash.edu), the main search endpoint was analyzed against Boolean-based SQL Injection (SQLi) vectors. While a robust Web Application Firewall (WAF) initially blocked automated testing, a successful perimeter bypass was executed to directly evaluate the backend application logic. The assessment concluded that the search parameter is Not Vulnerable due to its underlying enterprise search indexing architecture.
2. Target & Endpoint Discovery

The primary target was the global search form interface. A legitimate intercepted HTTP request revealed the following structure:

    Target URL: https://www.monash.edu/search

    Target Parameter: q (Search query string)

    Context Parameter: collection=mon~sp-main-search (Indicating the use of a pre-indexed enterprise search collection rather than a raw relational database query).

3. Technical Obstacles & Solutions (The Troubleshooting Journey)
❌ Obstacle 1: Perimeter Block via WAF (Cloudflare Bot Management)

Initial attempts to fuzz the endpoint using traditional CLI automated tools resulted in immediate HTTP 400 (Bad Request) responses with a Size: 0 body.

    Root Cause: Cloudflare utilizes advanced bot mitigation strategies, tracking the dynamic __cf_bm cookie and performing JA3/JA4 TLS Fingerprinting to detect and drop connections originating from non-browser network stacks.

    Solution: Integrated the OWASP ZAP suite with Selenium WebDriver inside a Kali Linux environment. Installed the system-native geckodriver (/usr/bin/geckodriver) to orchestrate a legitimate, fully-interactive instance of Firefox. Solving the initial anti-bot challenge manually allowed ZAP to inherit a legitimate TLS signature and a trusted session.

❌ Obstacle 2: HSTS Enforcement on Intercepting Proxy

Upon forcing the automated browser traffic through ZAP's local intercepting proxy (127.0.0.1:8080), Firefox blocked the connection entirely with the following error:

    “Software is Preventing Firefox From Safely Connecting to This Site”

    Root Cause: The target domain enforces strict HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS). Because the freshly launched automated Firefox instance did not trust ZAP’s generated Root CA, it actively blocked the connection to prevent a perceived Man-in-the-Middle (MitM) condition.

    Solution: Navigated to ZAP's modern configuration layout under Network -> Server Certificates, exported the ZAP Root CA (zap_root_ca.cer), and manually imported it into Firefox's certificate manager under the Authorities tab, granting explicit trust to identify websites.

4. Fuzzing Phase & Response Analysis

With the proxy chain properly configured and the WAF successfully bypassed, the OWASP ZAP Fuzzer was deployed to inject common SQLi bypass payloads into the q parameter. A URL Encode processor rule was enforced to guarantee payload compatibility.

The following payloads were tested against the baseline query:

    ' OR 1=1-- - (True condition)

    ' OR '1'='1 (True condition)

    ' && 1=2-- - (False condition)

    '//AND//1=2-- - (Space-bypass / False condition)

    ' AND 1 LIKE 2-- - (Alternative operator / False condition)

