5. Definitive Technical Diagnosis

Analyzing the Size column from the Fuzzer results yielded two critical insights:

    Absolute Size Uniformity: All 5 SQLi logical variants (both mathematically true and false arguments) returned responses with the exact same byte length. If the parameter were interacting with a raw SQL relational backend (e.g., MySQL, PostgreSQL), a false statement like AND 1 LIKE 2 would have restricted rows entirely, resulting in either a 0-result page size difference or a database syntax error (HTTP 500).

    Character Abstraction Layer: The application utilizes an enterprise index engine (such as Funnelback or Solr, as indicated by the collection parameter) that treats special control characters (', --, AND, OR) as literal text or throws them out as stop-words. Because the engine strips the operational logic of the payload, the backend effectively ran a plain-text search on the word "cybersecurity" for all instances, rendering an identical HTML tree across every payload.

6. Conclusion & Key Takeaways

The endpoint is definitively classified as a False Positive / Not Vulnerable to SQL Injection.
Key Achievements:

    Infrastructure Mastery: Successfully configured a local Selenium + Geckodriver framework inside Kali Linux to bridge advanced browser automation with OWASP ZAP.

    WAF Evasion: Bypassed restrictive Cloudflare JA3/JA4 TLS fingerprinting by mimicking authentic client connection states.

    Data-Driven Proof: Evaluated backend immunity by cross-referencing precise HTTP response byte alterations rather than relying on automated scanning heuristics.
